# The caveats section is located by a `^## ` heading match, and a README checked out with CRLF
# line endings reports as having no such heading at all -- so the entrypoint aborts with
# `CaveatsSectionMissing` on a file that plainly contains it. Verified. This is not cosmetic:
# it is a stranger on Windows being told the honesty section is missing when it is not.
README.md text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# `data/benign.jsonl` is 130.4 MB, measured 2026-08-30, and GitHub refuses any single file over
# 100 MB on push. It ships through Git LFS, decided by Fabricio Ceolin on 2026-08-30: this
# repository is experimental work for one reader, so the free tier's bandwidth is a limit nobody
# reaches, and in exchange the file stays REAL TEXT in the working tree -- which is the whole
# reason AD-1 commits the corpus rather than rebuilding it. Compression to 15.6 MB was smaller
# and would have taken that away from every reader at once.
#
# Named file by file rather than `data/*.jsonl`, and that is not tidiness: `attack.jsonl` is
# 7.2 MB and packs to 1.7 MB in ordinary git, where it stays diffable on the web and outside the
# LFS quota. The binding limit on the free tier is STORAGE, which does not reset and which GitHub
# does not garbage-collect, so every megabyte tracked here is permanent.
#
# `-text` overrides the `*.jsonl text eol=lf` above for this path, because LFS carries bytes and
# a line-ending conversion applied on top of a pointer file is a corruption of both.
#
# `corpus/storage.py` is what refuses a build whose corpus this declaration does not cover, and
# `corpus/manifest.py` is what tells a reader holding a pointer that they are holding one.
data/benign.jsonl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
